>   And there are even more, that haven't been mentioned in this discussion,
> like ri, ra and ru, (basically the most recently mentioned thing, something
> mentioned further back, and something even further back) vo'a, vo'e, vo'i,
> vo'o, and vo'u (the first through fifth sumti associated with the main
> bridi in this utterance), ti, ta, tu, ("pointing" indicators to things in
> the real world -- this, that, that over yonder), ways to refer to the
> sentences you are actually saying, etc.  Refer to chapter  7 of the grammar
> for the complete list and details. The equivalent of pronouns in lojban are
> complicated enough to warrant an entire chapter.
